---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { site } from "../data/site";
---

<Base title="Books" description="GPU Engineering: AI Inference and System Design (Packt), LLMOps and What is LLMOps (O'Reilly).">
  <section class="page">
    <p class="label">Books</p>
    <h1>The durable layer.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      LinkedIn is the question. The book is the reference. The course is the transformation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <article class="card book">
      <img
        class="book-cover"
        src="/assets/books/gpu-engineering.png"
        alt="Placeholder cover for GPU Engineering: a dragon on a GPU. Guess the cover."
        width="864"
        height="1152"
      />
      <div>
      <p class="label">Packt · almost finished</p>
      <h2>GPU Engineering: AI Inference and System Design</h2>
      <p>
        Modern AI workloads don't fail at the prompt, they fail at the systems boundary.
        This book covers the anatomy of high-throughput inference serving. It starts where
        traffic hits: concurrency at the gateway versus the engine, multi-tenant isolation,
        and the divide between the control plane and the data plane. From there it works
        down to bare metal: interconnect fabric, silicon, compilers, and kernels.
      </p>
      <p>
        Written for systems architects, infrastructure leads, and platform engineers who
        have to design the fleet rather than consume an endpoint.
      </p>
      <p>
        Almost finished. Launch is end of 2026 or early 2027. The
        <a href={site.links.maven}>Maven course</a> is a compressed version.
      </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="card book" style="margin-top: 2rem">
      <img
        class="book-cover"
        src="/assets/books/llmops.jpg"
        alt="Cover of LLMOps: Managing Large Language Models in Production, O'Reilly"
        width="800"
        height="1050"
      />
      <div>
      <p class="label">O'Reilly · written 2023-2024 · published 2025</p>
      <h2>LLMOps: Managing Large Language Models in Production</h2>
      <p>
        How language models actually get into production: evaluation, serving, cost, and the
        operational path from a demo to a system. Two years of writing, 2023-2024. Finished
        in December 2024, published in 2025. In English, now translated into Korean,
        Japanese, Russian, and Simplified Chinese.
      </p>
      <p><a class="btn" href={site.links.llmops}>LLMOps on O'Reilly</a></p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="card book" style="margin-top: 2rem">
      <img
        class="book-cover"
        src="/assets/books/what-is-llmops.jpg"
        alt="Cover of What is LLMOps, O'Reilly"
        width="800"
        height="1200"
      />
      <div>
      <p class="label">O'Reilly · report · written 2023 · published 2024</p>
      <h2>What is LLMOps</h2>
      <p>
        A separate O'Reilly report. The short brief on what LLMOps is, not the production book.
        Written in 2023, published in 2024.
      </p>
      <p><a class="btn" href={site.links.llmopsWhat}>What is LLMOps on O'Reilly</a></p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </section>
</Base>
